1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It's known as the most powerful handgun in the world.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Made famous by Clint Eastwood in the Dirty Harry movies.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
It is one of the biggest, baddest guns ever made.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Nearly unlimited firepower available with the pull of a trigger.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, the Magnum on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,547
On a gun range at Fort Benning, Georgia, members of the U.S.

7
00:00:37,548 --> 00:00:41,320
Army's elite marksmanship team

8
00:00:41,321 --> 00:00:44,500
fire one of the most powerful weapons in their arsenal.

9
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:52,440
These are handmade magnum rifles, specially fitted to the body of each shooter.

10
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:59,620
The oversized guns and the souped-up shells they fire are unique in the world of firearms.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,520
They are Magnums.

12
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:11,020
The targets in their gun sights are small because they are 600 yards away, a third of a mile.

13
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,740
You will have 20 minutes to shoot 20 well-aimed shots.

14
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:18,640
You may commence firing.

15
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:26,560
The recoil is probably three times as much as an M16,

16
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:30,700
and if you lay down there for about 40 minutes in a team match in 100-degree weather,

17
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:33,460
you start to notice the weight of it and the recoil gets to you,

18
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,900
so it's a little more mentally taxing, I think, to stay on top of it.

19
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:44,360
Besides their obvious power, one of the things that makes these weapons so remarkable

20
00:01:44,361 --> 00:01:48,180
is the bullets travel at nearly three times the speed of sound,

21
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:51,000
much faster than regular rounds.

22
00:01:51,001 --> 00:02:00,220
when spotters view the targets downrange they can actually see the bullets cut through the air

23
00:02:00,221 --> 00:02:02,520
something not possible with other guns

24
00:02:02,521 --> 00:02:14,100
the army places a high priority on its marksmanship team so much so that each gun is handmade and

25
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:19,120
every shell is hand loaded by army personnel to ensure the highest quality

26
00:02:19,121 --> 00:02:27,520
The term magnum in the world of firearms means more power to push a bullet at a higher rate

27
00:02:27,521 --> 00:02:33,860
of speed.

28
00:02:33,861 --> 00:02:38,000
Many people believe only large caliber handguns are magnums.

29
00:02:38,001 --> 00:02:40,640
The caliber is determined by the size of the bullet.

30
00:02:40,641 --> 00:02:47,640
A .44 caliber gun, for example, has a bore that is .44 hundredths of an inch in diameter.

31
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:57,640
In reality, any gun, pistol or rifle that fires a bullet with extra gunpowder in his cartridge

32
00:02:57,641 --> 00:03:00,640
is considered a magnum.

33
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:07,640
It's a generic term that can be applied to any weapon, handgun or rifle,

34
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:09,960
loaded with more powerful rounds.

35
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:16,760
The term magnum has come to mean something very, very powerful and very, very strong.

36
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:21,140
As a matter of fact, it's because of cartridges and firearms that it has this connotation.

37
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:29,800
In modern times, the gun's popularity can be traced directly to a famous movie line from the 1970s.

38
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:32,860
The actor was Clint Eastwood.

39
00:03:32,861 --> 00:03:34,660
The movie was Dirty Harry.

40
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:39,340
Movie director and firearms aficionado John Milius wrote the line.

41
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:46,620
This is a .44 Magnum, the most powerful handgun in the world,

42
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:49,580
and it will blow your head clean off.

43
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:54,640
Now, I don't know whether we fired five or six,

44
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,620
but the question you've got to ask yourself is, are you lucky, punk?

45
00:03:58,621 --> 00:04:09,420
But the desire for more power in a gun goes farther back in history than fighting crime

46
00:04:09,421 --> 00:04:12,522
on the streets of San Francisco.

47
00:04:12,523 --> 00:04:18,240
Packing more powerful loads was first developed in the 19th

48
00:04:18,241 --> 00:04:24,500
century in Britain, when big game hunters removed the bullets from gun shells, added more grains of

49
00:04:24,501 --> 00:04:31,500
gunpowder and then resealed the bullet to the cartridge.

50
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:35,060
The result? Sometimes they got more power in the gun.

51
00:04:35,061 --> 00:04:40,300
Sometimes the gun blew up in their face.

52
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:44,400
The reason why they wanted more power in a gun was simple.

53
00:04:44,401 --> 00:04:46,400
More force behind a bullet.

54
00:04:46,401 --> 00:04:52,680
Any sized bullet meant more striking power on a target.

55
00:04:52,681 --> 00:04:56,400
This, in turn, gave them a better chance of bringing down a charging animal

56
00:04:56,401 --> 00:04:58,960
before the animal had a chance to reach them.

57
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:04,780
These overloaded shells and the guns that fired them

58
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:07,340
were early versions of today's Magnums.

59
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:11,720
The guns were often called horse pistols

60
00:05:11,721 --> 00:05:15,680
because they were so bulky they could only be carried in the saddle of a horse.

61
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:22,180
Well, probably one of the most extreme examples of the big, large-caliber horse pistol

62
00:05:22,181 --> 00:05:24,440
was the British Model 1842 Lancer pistol.

63
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:26,140
It was a real hunker.

64
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,560
It was about .70 caliber.

65
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,200
It fired a carbine load of powder,

66
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:34,300
which is about a .65 grain charge,

67
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:36,780
and it was a real, real wrist breaker.

68
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,900
But it was probably guaranteed to stop anybody

69
00:05:39,901 --> 00:05:41,560
if you hit them in the right area.

70
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,600
The gun itself was so large, so unwieldy,

71
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:45,680
and so uncomfortable to shoot

72
00:05:45,681 --> 00:05:48,180
that the soldiers themselves really didn't like it.

73
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:50,180
They were issued to the Lancers and the Light Brigade,

74
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:51,880
but as many of them threw them away

75
00:05:51,881 --> 00:05:54,780
and availed themselves of Colt or Adams revolvers.

76
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,460
In the United States,

77
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:01,880
the man known for pushing the limits of power in a gun

78
00:06:01,881 --> 00:06:03,200
was Samuel Walker.

79
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:07,240
He was a member of the U.S. Army's regiment of mounted rifles

80
00:06:07,241 --> 00:06:10,400
and was on his way to help start a new group of lawmen,

81
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:12,020
the Texas Rangers.

82
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,940
Teaming with fledgling gunmaker Samuel Colt,

83
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:21,580
they created a six-shot revolver with a nine-inch barrel

84
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:24,820
that could fire the most powerful round in the world.

85
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:32,160
They called the gun, which fired a .44 caliber slug, the Walker Colt.

86
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:35,760
Only 1,000 Walker Colts were made.

87
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:39,300
But when the gun was introduced in 1846,

88
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:42,440
it sent shockwaves across the American frontier.

89
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:50,680
They devised this humongous, almost 5-pound .44 caliber revolver

90
00:06:50,681 --> 00:06:53,940
It was made by the Eli Whitney at Whitneyville.

91
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:59,860
And it fired, gosh, the powder charge was just about as big as that used in a military rifle.

92
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:03,840
It was about a 60 grain powder charge behind a big .44 caliber ball.

93
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:08,380
Six shot, very, very effective, very, very popular gun.

94
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:19,500
The new .44 caliber Walker Colt was the most powerful production revolver ever made until the 1980s.

95
00:07:19,501 --> 00:07:24,700
The old Colt Walker, still packed, more muzzle velocity, more muzzle energy.

96
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:31,360
And that .44 Magnum that Clint Eastwood, just dirty hairy, held in the hands of that Model 29 Smith & West.

97
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:38,460
In order for the gun to function, Walker and Colt designed a larger and stronger frame

98
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:42,440
that could withstand the explosive charge of the heavily packed bullets.

99
00:07:42,441 --> 00:07:51,340
the result was a loud violent revolver that fired six shots instead of the standard five

100
00:07:51,341 --> 00:07:56,980
it was lethal at 100 yards a much greater distance than most handguns of the time

101
00:07:56,981 --> 00:08:05,260
might say it was like bringing a howitzer to a knife fight it was devastating to the enemy the

102
00:08:05,261 --> 00:08:09,220
first time they encountered it used to have a real good chance that if somebody was hit with

103
00:08:09,221 --> 00:08:10,320
he was going down.

104
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:13,860
The gun was a favorite of the Texas Rangers.

105
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:18,320
The Rangers used the weapons in their fight with warring Comanche Indians,

106
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:22,920
pushing the border with the natives back 300 miles in two years.

107
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:29,300
Until then, the fight between the Rangers and the natives was practically a standoff.

108
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:35,300
Walker and Ranger Jack Hayes owed their lives to the firepower of the gun.

109
00:08:35,301 --> 00:08:42,820
Jack Coffey Hayes and Sam Walker holed up in that little canyon outside of Utopia, Texas,

110
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:46,580
fighting off dozens of Comanches with just a pair of revolvers.

111
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:51,180
Normally, in any other fight, those two guys would have been instant history.

112
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:57,660
But the power of the Walker cult could not save its inventor from an early demise.

113
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:02,780
Samuel Walker was fighting in the Mexican-American War in 1847.

114
00:09:02,781 --> 00:09:10,780
Walker led a group of 50 Texas Rangers against a stronghold of Mexican soldiers in the border town of Formantea.

115
00:09:10,781 --> 00:09:14,680
You see 500 of Santa Ana's best trained regular men.

116
00:09:14,681 --> 00:09:19,780
These aren't volunteers, these are regulars that Walker rides in on.

117
00:09:19,781 --> 00:09:24,780
These are the same guys that brought the Alamo down in 1836.

118
00:09:24,781 --> 00:09:30,780
And Walker just shows up with a tenth of the size in men and runs these guys right out of town.

119
00:09:30,781 --> 00:09:34,900
I mean, just completely scares them, turns them heel to toe, and they're out.

120
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:43,040
To add insult to injury, Walker fired the Mexicans' cannons at the fleeing army by using his Walker Colts.

121
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:47,060
He wheels the guns around at the retreating Mexicans,

122
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,460
and he pulls out this new revolver that he got from Colt,

123
00:09:50,780 --> 00:09:54,780
and he cocks it and he fires the full blast right into the breach of the cannon,

124
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:58,980
knowing that the fire from the charge is going to light off the round in the cannon.

125
00:09:58,981 --> 00:10:00,380
And sure enough, it does.

126
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:05,620
And he takes both guns and he's firing these artillery pieces that the Mexicans left behind.

127
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:11,220
Outside of town, the Mexicans realized that a much smaller force,

128
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:14,340
armed with loud and powerful handguns, had rotted them.

129
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:19,400
They returned and began fighting bloody hand-to-hand combat.

130
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:26,960
One of the Mexicans gets on a balcony across the town square and he fires a musket shotgun.

131
00:10:26,961 --> 00:10:33,240
And one of the many bullets that that thing fires creases Walker's head and also his upper chest.

132
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:38,160
And as he takes his fatal wound and slumps down to the pavement and doorframe of a house,

133
00:10:38,660 --> 00:10:41,360
he tells the men, don't give up.

134
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:42,920
Keep fighting without me.

135
00:10:42,921 --> 00:10:49,700
And he turns his guns over to one of the troopers in his command, William Ashbaugh, and then expires.

136
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:55,180
The death of the much-loved Walker sent the Rangers on a rampage.

137
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:56,800
They destroyed the town.

138
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:58,120
and everything in it.

139
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:03,840
Walker was young in his mid-30s, very slight of build, blue eyes.

140
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:07,700
He was the bon vivant of military courage.

141
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:12,440
To learn that he died in battle like that, they went nuts.

142
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:15,420
It was the first time in American military history

143
00:11:15,421 --> 00:11:19,700
that you might say they stood a good ground for wartime atrocities.

144
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:22,180
They laid waste to the top.

145
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:24,520
The Mexicans were eventually pushed out.

146
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:26,480
No quarter was given.

147
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:28,500
You may as well have flown a black flag.

148
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:30,340
You weren't taking prisoners.

149
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:32,600
Everything got torched.

150
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:37,820
It was a real ugly scene in the mantle of the evening of Walker's death.

151
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:43,220
When we continue, the charge of wild beasts in Africa

152
00:11:43,221 --> 00:11:47,100
leads British gun companies to create the first true Magnum.

153
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:55,100
It took nearly 150 years to discover what happened to the guns Samuel Walker gave away when he died.

154
00:11:55,101 --> 00:11:59,780
They were eventually returned to his family and today are housed in a private gun collection.

155
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:01,960
The Magnum will be right back.

156
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:08,100
You're watching The Magnum on Modern Marvels.

157
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:16,600
The gun rooms at the Holland & Holland Firearms Company in London, England

158
00:12:16,601 --> 00:12:19,860
possess the distinct whisper of days gone by.

159
00:12:19,861 --> 00:12:27,580
trophies of game both large and small bagged by guns manufactured by Holland and Holland line the

160
00:12:27,581 --> 00:12:34,020
walls and we're probably more famous throughout the world for the manufacture of sporting rifles

161
00:12:34,021 --> 00:12:40,640
while our hunting rifles cover the whole spectrum so from deer stalking typically in Scotland or in

162
00:12:40,641 --> 00:12:47,820
the home counties right the way through to the largest game the African game the company is

163
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:50,080
nearly two hundred years old.

164
00:12:50,081 --> 00:12:54,020
Its effort to make extra powerful hunting rifles in the 1880s

165
00:12:54,021 --> 00:12:58,960
led to the development of the modern-day Magnum.

166
00:12:58,961 --> 00:13:01,200
It was the age of Sherlock Holmes.

167
00:13:01,201 --> 00:13:04,940
The Industrial Revolution had gripped the city.

168
00:13:04,941 --> 00:13:07,940
Smokestacks from factories turned the sky black

169
00:13:07,941 --> 00:13:09,140
with an obnoxious smog.

170
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:16,860
As the writer Oscar Wilde succinctly put it at the time,

171
00:13:16,861 --> 00:13:20,160
The nice thing about living in London is you can see the air you're breathing.

172
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:25,260
To escape from the unhealthy chaos of the city,

173
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:30,220
wealthy English gentlemen traveled to the wilds of Africa to hunt big game.

174
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:35,720
They called their adventures safaris, which lasted for months

175
00:13:35,721 --> 00:13:40,400
and had as many as a hundred people trudging through the jungles hunting dangerous animals.

176
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:44,000
Victorian hunters were shooting just about everything that moved.

177
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:46,680
There wasn't too much regulation back then.

178
00:13:46,860 --> 00:13:54,440
elephants, tigers, lions, various types of antelope, giraffes, rhinoceroses,

179
00:13:54,441 --> 00:13:57,360
basically pretty much anything that they can encounter.

180
00:13:57,361 --> 00:14:01,700
And they'd have guns, a number of different guns with them of varying calibers and varying powers

181
00:14:01,701 --> 00:14:03,860
to deal with whatever creature they came across.

182
00:14:03,861 --> 00:14:10,520
But the hunters on these expeditions quickly realized they had a problem, a big problem.

183
00:14:10,521 --> 00:14:18,060
Many of the guns they used were ineffective against the largest and most dangerous animals in Africa.

184
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:25,080
There's no doubt that in those days, the reliability of the rifles, although good,

185
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:29,720
was nothing like the quality of rifles and ammunition today.

186
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:34,740
One could not rely so much on the ammunition that you had in those days.

187
00:14:34,820 --> 00:14:39,500
Certainly the calibers were there to do the business in any African situation.

188
00:14:39,501 --> 00:14:46,220
misfires and the failure of the rifle and the ammunition is not a situation you want when

189
00:14:46,221 --> 00:14:49,660
something dangerous and angry is charging you.

190
00:14:49,661 --> 00:14:53,100
A simple hunting rifle used back in England to

191
00:14:53,101 --> 00:14:57,769
shoot boar or quail was no match for a charging elephant.

192
00:14:57,770 --> 00:15:00,800
Many hunters were killed, not because

193
00:15:00,801 --> 00:15:05,391
they were bad shots, but because their guns could not do the job.

194
00:15:05,392 --> 00:15:07,580
When you're chasing a lion or a

195
00:15:09,311 --> 00:15:10,292
or an elephant.

196
00:15:10,293 --> 00:15:18,339
The emphasis, of course, is to stop the animal as quickly as possible so that you protect yourself from any kind of danger.

197
00:15:18,340 --> 00:15:26,583
And therefore, the big game hunters were continually searching for a cartridge that would have this tremendous stopping power.

198
00:15:26,584 --> 00:15:35,480
They'd essentially like a cartridge that was as strong as a freight train, that when it hit an animal, it stopped it dead in its tracks.

199
00:15:35,481 --> 00:15:42,020
So they needed bigger bullets and larger cartridges, larger cartridge cases, to get the effect that they wanted.

200
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:51,380
So if you're shooting an elephant or a tiger or something like that, you needed a large, slow-moving, heavy bullet to try to take them down.

201
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:56,340
Once they realized what the need was, others then followed on.

202
00:15:56,740 --> 00:16:03,540
And the sport hunter followed in the footsteps of the first people who were literally opening up the country and discovering what was there.

203
00:16:03,541 --> 00:16:10,200
So this information then came back and the gun makers set about meeting the needs of the sportsmen.

204
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:15,140
So this is how the development of these new calibers came about,

205
00:16:15,660 --> 00:16:19,180
moving from the bore rifles to the express rifles.

206
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:27,760
To give the hunters a sporting chance, Holland & Holland developed in the mid-1880s an express rifle,

207
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:32,860
named after express trains that ran from the English countryside into the city,

208
00:16:32,861 --> 00:16:34,260
without stopping.

209
00:16:36,320 --> 00:16:37,940
Express rifles come about

210
00:16:37,941 --> 00:16:39,660
because of the velocity

211
00:16:39,661 --> 00:16:41,500
at which the bullet was propelled.

212
00:16:42,080 --> 00:16:43,360
A more effective means

213
00:16:43,361 --> 00:16:45,100
of sealing the chamber was found

214
00:16:45,101 --> 00:16:47,000
and it was possible to raise the pressures

215
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:49,000
and develop higher velocities

216
00:16:49,001 --> 00:16:50,480
and hence the express.

217
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:53,320
And it's possible to produce as much energy

218
00:16:53,321 --> 00:16:55,800
in a bullet by speeding it faster

219
00:16:55,801 --> 00:16:58,180
so that you could have a smaller projectile

220
00:16:58,181 --> 00:16:59,600
traveling at a higher velocity

221
00:16:59,601 --> 00:17:02,420
but would still deliver the same impact energy.

222
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:09,860
When introduced to African safari hunters, the gun was an immediate success.

223
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:16,860
Hunters no longer worried that large game could take a bullet and keep coming.

224
00:17:16,861 --> 00:17:20,860
Express rifles were forged with super strong double barrels.

225
00:17:20,861 --> 00:17:25,860
Because of this added strength, cartridges packed with high loads of gunpowder

226
00:17:25,861 --> 00:17:30,860
could be fired safely from each barrel at the same time.

227
00:17:30,861 --> 00:17:37,680
The result was a gun that could shoot a bullet nearly 50% faster than traditional hunting rifles, meaning more deadly shots.

228
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:47,020
The method of shooting most commonly in those days was to literally puncture a large hole in its vitals and let the blood run out.

229
00:17:47,580 --> 00:17:54,480
So when an animal was shot, it was quite typical that there would be a follow-up until this chain of events took place.

230
00:17:55,400 --> 00:18:01,280
Now the reason for this was that the projectiles that were being used in the main soft lead

231
00:18:01,281 --> 00:18:06,760
were just incapable of penetrating very dense tissue such as bone,

232
00:18:06,761 --> 00:18:10,440
so it was more prudent then to shoot into the softer area of the beast.

233
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:16,420
With the advent of the express cartridges and much higher velocity from the bullets

234
00:18:16,421 --> 00:18:23,600
and alloying the bullets and then later on putting cupronickel jackets over the lead ball itself

235
00:18:23,601 --> 00:18:25,840
and turning the ball into a bullet,

236
00:18:26,380 --> 00:18:29,060
then it was possible to get more penetration.

237
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:33,520
And then smaller calibers, traveling at higher velocities,

238
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,040
were able to put a beast down straight away.

239
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:39,820
It was sometime during the late 19th century

240
00:18:39,821 --> 00:18:43,880
that the term magnum was first used to describe high-powered guns.

241
00:18:45,700 --> 00:18:49,140
No one is really sure who first attached the word to the weapons,

242
00:18:49,141 --> 00:18:53,160
but it's certain the word was taken from the oversized bottle of French champagne.

243
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:59,640
The word magnum comes from the Latin magnus, meaning great.

244
00:19:00,380 --> 00:19:06,560
The Rothschilds have been using the term magnum as one of their largest bottles of wine or champagne.

245
00:19:06,780 --> 00:19:09,420
It's almost a half a gallon of champagne.

246
00:19:09,421 --> 00:19:16,240
When they were casting about for a catchy name to give this new high-powered cartridge that they've developed for a handgun,

247
00:19:16,760 --> 00:19:18,160
they settled on the magnum.

248
00:19:18,161 --> 00:19:25,780
It's believed that English hunters who opened up their cartridges and put in more gunpowder

249
00:19:25,781 --> 00:19:29,920
to create rounds that packed more punch could have coined the term.

250
00:19:29,921 --> 00:19:38,460
But in the coming decades, not many gun companies jumped on the Magnum bandwagon.

251
00:19:38,461 --> 00:19:44,520
Even the British military was disinterested and passed on buying any of the guns.

252
00:19:44,521 --> 00:19:49,680
These magnum rifles were built to withstand the extra explosive charge of a magnum shell.

253
00:19:49,681 --> 00:19:56,400
The guns were heavier and more difficult to carry into battle.

254
00:19:56,401 --> 00:20:03,320
By the turn of the century, only big game hunters had any interest in high powered guns.

255
00:20:03,321 --> 00:20:08,760
The most famous big game hunter of the day was US President Theodore Roosevelt.

256
00:20:08,761 --> 00:20:15,040
When he left office in 1909, Roosevelt mounted the greatest African safari ever seen, complete

257
00:20:15,041 --> 00:20:17,520
with an arsenal of high-powered weaponry.

258
00:20:17,521 --> 00:20:23,020
He wanted to mount a safari like nobody had ever seen before, and he pretty well did.

259
00:20:23,021 --> 00:20:24,940
It was quite an extravaganza.

260
00:20:24,941 --> 00:20:31,660
He had a Holland & Holland 500 450, very powerful double rifle, 3040 Craig, which was a military

261
00:20:31,661 --> 00:20:34,460
caliber and that was very popular.

262
00:20:34,461 --> 00:20:38,300
Basically he took enough guns with him to deal with just about anything he would encounter.

263
00:20:38,301 --> 00:20:41,460
And he pretty well shot it. He sent back lots and lots of specimens,

264
00:20:41,461 --> 00:20:44,900
a number of which are in museums in the United States.

265
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:49,540
World War I put an end to the construction of Magnum guns,

266
00:20:49,541 --> 00:20:51,540
as well as most sporting rifles.

267
00:20:52,740 --> 00:20:57,460
British gun makers instead set their sights on more pressing wartime issues.

268
00:20:58,340 --> 00:21:03,740
Sporting rifle manufacture came to a complete end by 1915.

269
00:21:03,741 --> 00:21:16,954
But our knowledge and expertise with ballistics and rifles in general involved us in the development of a shotgun that fired a special projectile for ripping holes in

270
00:21:16,955 --> 00:21:19,740
the coverings of Zeppelin airships.

271
00:21:21,960 --> 00:21:28,740
When we continue, an American gunmaker captures the massive power of a magnum rifle in a handgun.

272
00:21:28,741 --> 00:21:36,360
Roosevelt Safari of 1909 was a major media event.

273
00:21:36,361 --> 00:21:42,260
Besides 100 staff members, two dozen journalists traveled with the former president to chronicle the journey.

