276
00:21:53,120 --> 00:21:58,480
In the early 1930s, super-powered British hunting rifles named Magnums

277
00:21:58,481 --> 00:22:03,240
caught the attention of officials at an American gun company called Smith & Wesson.

278
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:09,860
Avid hunters, the Wessons, saw how the extra power of these guns

279
00:22:09,861 --> 00:22:12,560
could be of interest to American hunters in the West.

280
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:18,000
But Smith & Wesson was best known for its handguns,

281
00:22:18,001 --> 00:22:19,680
which were famous throughout the world.

282
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:23,291
Company owners D.B.

283
00:22:23,292 --> 00:22:27,280
and Victor Wesson wondered if the powerful charge of a magnum rifle

284
00:22:27,281 --> 00:22:28,900
could be harnessed in a handgun.

285
00:22:30,380 --> 00:22:33,580
Doug Wesson was an outdoorsman, a big game hunter,

286
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:37,300
and he was not afraid of recoil.

287
00:22:37,980 --> 00:22:43,920
Victor Wesson, who was plant superintendent at the time,

288
00:22:44,420 --> 00:22:48,660
said, oh, nobody would buy that gun because it's going to recoil so much

289
00:22:48,661 --> 00:22:50,900
and you won't be able to handle it.

290
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:53,760
It's far greater than anyone's going to be interested in.

291
00:22:53,761 --> 00:22:58,100
But the nation was in the grips of a crime wave.

292
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:01,560
Gangsters and so-called motorized bandits

293
00:23:01,561 --> 00:23:04,320
who prowled the country in fast-moving cars

294
00:23:04,321 --> 00:23:06,160
were terrorizing the public.

295
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:10,960
At the time, Smith & Wesson's best-selling revolver

296
00:23:10,961 --> 00:23:12,040
was the .38 Special,

297
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:15,520
a favorite among police agencies and criminals alike.

298
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:20,880
The customary velocity of the .38 Special Service cartridge

299
00:23:20,881 --> 00:23:29,560
around that time was 158 grain lead round nose bullet going about 850 feet per second.

300
00:23:29,561 --> 00:23:36,940
And this just was not enough to defeat the bulletproof vest or penetrate the auto bodies of the period.

301
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:43,540
Cops were asking for more power in their handguns to battle the well-armed bad guys.

302
00:23:44,180 --> 00:23:47,420
Smith and Wesson believed a Magnum handgun could be the answer.

303
00:23:47,421 --> 00:23:54,420
The company began experimenting with a stronger frame for its .38 Special.

304
00:23:54,421 --> 00:24:00,420
After two years, they came upon the right mix of steel and strength.

305
00:24:00,421 --> 00:24:07,420
In 1935, Smith & Wesson introduced the .357 Magnum revolver,

306
00:24:07,421 --> 00:24:12,420
a louder, more violent, more expensive cousin of the .38 Special.

307
00:24:12,421 --> 00:24:20,380
When they built the guns and started to offer them, it was a custom-built firearm.

308
00:24:20,940 --> 00:24:24,060
Now, we're talking about the time when we were in a depression,

309
00:24:24,640 --> 00:24:29,260
where the average handgun at Smith & Wesson was selling for around $25 to $30.

310
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,200
The .357 Magnum, when it was introduced, was introduced at $60.

311
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:41,520
The first .357 Magnum off the manufacturing line, registration number one,

312
00:24:41,521 --> 00:24:45,360
was sent to J. Edgar Hoover, head of the FBI.

313
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:52,240
Hoover liked the gun so much that he instructed his field officers to test it

314
00:24:52,241 --> 00:24:55,400
and order one for themselves if the power of the weapon suited them.

315
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:03,340
Like magnum rifles, the handgun had an incredible kick compared to normal guns of the time.

316
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:09,980
The muzzle flash was tremendous, and the noise it made was enough to scare a criminal into submission.

317
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:15,300
Several police departments adopted the Magnum as their service revolver.

318
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:30,280
This is a late vintage .357 Magnum, made probably in the 1970s, but quite similar to the guns made in the 1930s.

319
00:25:30,281 --> 00:25:33,660
This was built, again, on a large frame.

320
00:25:35,660 --> 00:25:39,300
The original ones were virtually custom made.

321
00:25:39,301 --> 00:25:41,780
They came with a registration number.

322
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:50,060
The registered Smith & Wesson Magnums are often thought to have been the finest revolvers ever made.

323
00:25:50,600 --> 00:25:53,920
Unlike its predecessor, the Magnum and Express Rifles,

324
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:59,480
Smith & Wesson had a tough time filling all the orders for the .357 Magnum handgun.

325
00:26:01,920 --> 00:26:05,960
Police officials liked the extra power of the gun and ordered thousands of them,

326
00:26:05,961 --> 00:26:09,040
but officers in the field didn't like the size and weight.

327
00:26:09,300 --> 00:26:17,200
So, many law enforcement agencies opted to stick with a .38 Special to keep their officers happy.

328
00:26:17,201 --> 00:26:23,940
The one place the Magnum did find acceptance was with big-game hunters.

329
00:26:23,941 --> 00:26:32,420
The same people who had needed a bigger and better rifle a half century earlier now flocked to the Magnum handgun.

330
00:26:32,421 --> 00:26:43,500
All of the firearms enthusiasts, being a handgun hunter, competitive shooter, were interested in trying this world's most powerful handgun.

331
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:55,360
The big game hunters, after Doug Wesson took it out west and successfully killed a moose and an elk and an antelope with it, wanted to try the gun and big game.

332
00:26:55,361 --> 00:27:02,360
Hunters loved being able to carry a handgun into the bush instead of a bulky rifle.

333
00:27:02,361 --> 00:27:11,360
It seemed the Magnum was relegated once again to bringing down big game in the American West and the wilds of Africa.

334
00:27:11,361 --> 00:27:22,360
A good example of the reverence for the gun is best surmised in a passage from a Smith & Wesson publication in 1938.

335
00:27:22,361 --> 00:27:25,569
A Catholic priest named Bernard R.

336
00:27:25,570 --> 00:27:31,420
Hubbard wrote to Smith & Wesson from his post in Nome, Alaska.

337
00:27:34,820 --> 00:27:43,780
He wanted the company to know that the new .357 Magnum handgun had saved the lives of his Eskimo parishioners during a recent walrus hunt.

338
00:27:43,781 --> 00:27:52,640
The hunting had been poor that year, and the Eskimos were in danger of starving unless a large walrus was brought down.

339
00:27:55,080 --> 00:28:03,860
Father Hubbard says, in part, the Eskimo hunters are very impressed with the Smith & Wesson 357 Magnum

340
00:28:03,861 --> 00:28:12,300
after seeing them in action on the walrus herd, as they are the most prized weapon any of us have ever had.

341
00:28:12,301 --> 00:28:18,940
Father Hubbard doesn't give the weight of the big bull, but as it was the largest bull this past season,

342
00:28:19,480 --> 00:28:24,080
and we know that 2,000 pounds is not an unusual weight for these animals,

343
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:35,560
we really feel that three bulls with three bullets definitely established the .357 Magnum as a big game revolver on its own right.

344
00:28:35,561 --> 00:28:44,320
As had happened before, a world war nearly made this newly minted magnum extinct.

345
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:48,480
Though the gun did have at least one big fan in the military.

346
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:55,420
General George Patton carried a Colt revolver on one hip and a magnum on the other.

347
00:28:56,100 --> 00:28:59,900
But American troops would never carry such an unwieldy gun into battle.

348
00:28:59,901 --> 00:29:09,100
Following the war, Smith & Wesson revived experiments with the Magnum-sized guns, believing

349
00:29:09,101 --> 00:29:13,760
more power was the future of weaponry.

350
00:29:13,761 --> 00:29:18,540
To push the limits of just how powerful a handgun the company could make, it constructed

351
00:29:18,541 --> 00:29:24,480
the gun that would become famous throughout the world, the .44 Magnum.

352
00:29:24,481 --> 00:29:27,840
The gun was first put on the market in 1956.

353
00:29:27,841 --> 00:29:31,800
It was huge by anyone's standards.

354
00:29:31,801 --> 00:29:37,800
It came in a variety of barrel lengths, from 4 inches up to 8 3 8 inches.

355
00:29:37,801 --> 00:29:45,680
It fired a .44 caliber slug more than 1,400 feet a second.

356
00:29:45,681 --> 00:29:51,980
Shooting a .44 Magnum, I think, depends a lot on who you are and what your psyche is

357
00:29:51,981 --> 00:29:54,140
about it.

358
00:29:54,141 --> 00:29:56,200
I've had people who shot it.

359
00:29:56,860 --> 00:30:00,940
My daughter, when she was nine, enjoyed shooting the .44 Magnum.

360
00:30:01,020 --> 00:30:01,960
She thought it was fun.

361
00:30:02,980 --> 00:30:05,820
I've had big men who were afraid to shoot it,

362
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:10,060
and when it would go off, almost drop it out of their hands.

363
00:30:10,780 --> 00:30:12,980
I grew up with big bore guns,

364
00:30:12,981 --> 00:30:16,860
and so to me, it's not uncomfortable to shoot it.

365
00:30:17,180 --> 00:30:18,540
It has a lot of recoil.

366
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:19,880
It has a lot of noise.

367
00:30:20,880 --> 00:30:23,400
But if you're used to it, and you're controlled,

368
00:30:23,401 --> 00:30:28,700
and you're mentally prepared, it's not as dramatic as most people think.

